bitcoin supply squeeze now the bitcoin  supply squeeze  is something that you've probably heard  us talk about we had will clemente on  the show on friday he was talking about  this as well  and a lot of folks in the comments are  asking what the hell is the supply  squeeze  how does it work so i'm going to do my  best to explain to you what the supply  squeeze is  and then we're going to look at some  updated information on it but the idea  behind the supply squeeze  is there are mainly three different  numbers that you need to be aware of  the first number is the total number of  bitcoin 21 million bitcoin will ever  exist  outside of the 21 million bitcoin there  are bitcoins in circulation meaning the  bitcoins that have now come into the  market  and are circulating around that is about  89  almost 90 percent of the 21 million so  about 18.7 18.8 million bitcoin just  below 18.8 million bitcoin are actually  in circulation 21 million total ever  18.8 million are in circulation  but of those that are in circulation we  can tell  on a blockchain exactly where they land  or where they are sitting and what we  find is that we can monitor  how many of those bitcoin of the 18.8  million in circulation are on exchanges  versus sitting in some sort of illiquid  entity  now the reason why being on an exchange  is important  is because the main way that people buy  and sell bitcoin  is on an exchange so if i don't have my  bitcoin on the exchange  i am unlikely to be selling the bitcoin  it also means that anyone who's looking  to buy bitcoin  can not buy my bitcoin because i don't  have my bitcoin on the exchange  so the more bitcoin that are off the  exchanges it means  the less bitcoin that are on the  exchange you don't have to be a  scientist or a genius to know that  now when we start to look at the data  what we can see is that  the illiquid supply the amount of  bitcoin  that is sitting in these entities where  they are not sitting on the exchanges  has hit an all-time high recently over  the weekend and so will  posted this photo on twitter and when  you look at it you can see on the left  side how many bitcoin are actually  sitting in the illiquid supply  and you can see the price on the right  now what is fascinating about this  is the fact that the illiquid supply  uh ends up being very tied to price  why if more and more bitcoin are coming  off of exchanges it means there's less  bitcoin on the exchanges to buy  as more people come into the market they  look to actually purchase  the bitcoin that is available but if  they don't have enough bitcoin to buy  they have to start paying more and more  so we can see on this chart here going  back to august 2020 bitcoin was sitting  down around  uh let's say ten thousand dollars give  or take  and you can see that with that gold uh  price uh schedule that illiquid supply  you can see  that the price and the liquid supply  both moved upwards now what that means  in practice if you're not looking at the  graph  is basically the idea that i have  bitcoin  and so did millions of people around the  world when the institutions showed up in  q3 and q4 of 2020  they said we would like to buy bitcoin  for ten thousand dollars and some people  sold them the bitcoin but not enough to  satisfy the demand of the institutions  so the institution said you know what  i'll pay twelve thousand dollars for  your bitcoin  now i'll pay 15 then 20. and at each  time they started to bid  more and more for bitcoin bitcoiner said  i'm not  selling about 56 give or take  of bitcoin has not moved in the last 12  months  even though the price had appreciated  hundreds of percent  it had drawn down 50 lots of volatility  but over 50 of bitcoin did not move in  the last year  and so what that means is no matter how  much they bid  from a price standpoint bitcoiners were  not willing to sell their bitcoin to  these new buyers  and so the institutions went from ten  thousand to fifteen to twenty  to twenty five thirty thirty five  thousand forty thousand  forty five fifty fifty five thousand  sixty thousand  at one point people were willing to pay  sixty four thousand 000 per bitcoin  well a lot of that was driven by the  fact that  when bitcoin was being bought on the  exchanges people were then pulling it  off so there was less and less bitcoin  on those exchanges and so you saw the  price continue to  appreciate we then obviously had a  market correction when you go  up hundreds of percent obviously you're  not going to just go up forever  and so when that market correction  occurred there was tons of liquidations  the price drew  drew down and we kind of settled around  30 to 40 thousand dollars  and so we've kind of been consolidating  sidewish for a while but recently as you  see in the chart here  you can see with that gold um kind of  line  that the illiquid supply has begun to  rise again  and so when the illiquid supply  continues to rise meaning that more and  more bitcoin is coming off of the  exchange than sitting in the illiquid  entities  it is a good indicator it is not a  guarantee but it is a good indicator  that the price will have to increase  over time  because there is a less supply available  that is where you get this idea of a  supply  shock as the supply gets smaller and  smaller and smaller the addressable  circulating supply as that shrinks down  new demand has less bitcoin available to  it to buy  and therefore if they want to increase  how much bitcoin they can buy they have  to bid a higher and higher price pulling  the entire market up  so that is how a supply shock works  you
 

